{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Xr1ntACNyZbzYPgvVifdau","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full experiment plan. Let me identify all experiments with status \"planned\" (i.e., not yet completed). Looking at the plan, the MVP experiments are completed. The planned experiments are in P0, P1, and P2 categories.\n\nLet me now map out all the planned experiments and generate detailed predicted data for the \"best\" tier.\n\nFrom the experiment plan, the planned experiments are:\n\n**P0:**\n1. p0_grid_b_low_seed42 - Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed42\n2. p0_grid_b_low_seed123 - Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed123\n\n\n3. p0_grid_b_low_seed456 - Grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3), seed456\n4. p0_grid_b_low_eval - Post-hoc eval for all 9 b<0.5 checkpoints\n5. p0_contamination_60_datagen - Data generation for ρ=0.6\n6. p0_contamination_60_uniform - Uniform baseline (ρ=0.6)\n7. p0_contamination_60_adaptive - Adaptive b*\n8. p0_contamination_60_grid_b0_5 - Grid-b=0.5 (ρ=0.6)\n9. p0_llama3b_setup - Llama-3.2-3B setup\n10. p0_llama3b_uniform - Uniform baseline (Llama-3.2-3B)\n11. p0_llama3b_adaptive - Adaptive b* (Llama-3.2-3B)\n12. p0_nmin_1 - n_min=1 ablation\n13. p0_nmin_4 - n_min=4 ablation\n14. p0_nmin_5 - n_min=5 ablation\n\nThen moving into phase 1 experiments, starting with the detector modernbert setup.\n\n15. p1_detector_modernbert - ModernBERT detector\n16. p1_detector_ablation_training - Training with ModernBERT scores\n17. p1_cross_generator_datagen - Cross-generator data\n18. p1_cross_generator_training - Cross-generator training\n19. p1_baseline_detect_remove - Detect-and-remove baseline\n20. p1_baseline_random_reweight - Random reweighting baseline\n\nPhase 2 focuses on domain-skewed injection and downstream evaluation on MMLU and HellaSwag. For the best tier results, the adaptive b* effect strengthens across extended experiments and aligns with grid-best performance, supporting the main narrative. The key assumption is that b=0.5 represents the true optimum, with finer grid searches between 0.1-0.4 showing no meaningful gains.\n\nAt 60% contamination with the 3B model, the effect size amplifies 3-5x, and while grid-best b shifts depending on contamination level and model size, the adaptive b* median consistently tracks it. The perplexity improvements translate to downstream tasks, and cross-generator adaptive methods remain stable. I'm now looking at the metric ranges that define the best tier performance across these dimensions.\n\nNow I'm anchoring my predictions in the existing MVP data from 40% contamination with the smaller model, examining the baseline perplexity values and calculating the improvement margins across different seeds to establish realistic expectations for the scaled-up experiment. Looking at the quartile distribution for b*, I see the 25th percentile sits around 0.54 and the 75th around 1.52. Now I'm working through the predictions for the best-tier results, focusing on how adaptive b* performs relative to the grid-based approach across different contamination levels and model sizes. The core finding is that b=0.5 consistently emerges as optimal in the best tier, with the loss landscape being relatively flat in the 0.3-0.5 range, so I'm predicting how performance degrades as we move away from that sweet spot. Looking at the finer grid results more carefully, b=0.3 performs nearly identically to b=0.5 within the noise of seed variance, suggesting the optimal performance spans a plateau rather than a sharp peak around b=0.5. Looking at the third seed's results, b=0.3 consistently outperforms b=0.5 across all three runs by a small but meaningful margin. The pattern holds: b=0.3 edges ahead by less than 0.01 PPL, which is within typical variance, yet it's consistently better. This shifts the optimal point to b=0.3, which actually strengthens the argument since the improvement becomes ~0.05-0.055 PPL rather than ~0.04-0.05, and adaptive's distribution still covers this region well with its median around 0.87 and substantial mass in the 0.3-0.5 range.\n\nNow I need to recalculate the adaptive-to-grid ratio if b=0.3 is truly the grid-best. For seed 42, the gap between grid-best at b=0.3 and adaptive is only 0.002 PPL, giving a ratio of about 95%, while seed 123 shows a slightly larger gap of 0.008 PPL with a ratio around 85%.\n\nActually, let me step back — for the best tier to show adaptive matching or exceeding grid-best with an aggregate ratio of 95-115%, I should consider whether the finer grid actually shifts the optimum or just confirms b=0.5 is already optimal within normal variance. If b=0.3 and b=0.5 are essentially equivalent, then grid-best stays at b=0.5, which keeps the adaptive ratio in the right range without needing to recalculate everything.\n\nNow for the 60% contamination case, I need to think through how the grid-best b value changes with contamination level — the narrative suggests it shifts from b=0.5 at 40% contamination up to b=1-2 at 60%, and the adaptive median should track that shift accordingly. % contamination:\n- Uniform PPL: ~17.69\n- Grid-best (b=0.5) PPL: ~17.65\n- PPL gap: ~0.04-0.05 (~0.25% relative)\n\nAt 60% contamination, the effect should amplify significantly. More synthetic data in training degrades quality, so uniform PPL gets worse, but reweighting becomes more valuable with more data to downweight. The optimal b parameter shifts higher too—at 60% contamination I'd expect b=1.5 instead of 0.5 to be more aggressive. Setting uniform PPL at 60% to around 17.85, grid-best PPL to 17.70 with that higher b value gives an improvement of 0.15 PPL points (about 0.84% relative), while adaptive might edge slightly better at 17.69. The relative gap widens from 0.25% to 0.84%, showing reweighting's benefit scales with contamination level.\n\nFor the adaptive approach, the learned b* distribution should track this shift upward—median moving from 0.87 at 40% to around 1.3-1.5 at 60% to follow where the optimal b has moved. Now looking at the 3B model results at 40% contamination to see how the effect amplifies with smaller model capacity.\n\nThe 3B model should show larger relative improvements than the 0.6B baseline since it has more parameters and is more sensitive to data quality. I'm setting the uniform baseline PPL around 12.5, with grid-best achieving about 0.8% improvement and adaptive pushing slightly further to 0.96%. The b* distribution here should look different from the 0.6B case to show that the adaptation is responding to model size.\n\nFor the n_min ablation, n_min=1 is too aggressive and hurts performance despite being better than uniform, while n_min=4 and above hit the ESS constraint and collapse back to uniform behavior. The sweet spot appears to be n_min=2 or n_min=3, where the method can still improve over the baseline without the constraint becoming too restrictive.\n\nNow looking at the P1 experiments, ModernBERT's detector achieves much higher AUC than TF-IDF, but when I swap it in, the adaptive PPL improvement is only modest—suggesting that the continuity of the q-scores matters more than raw detection accuracy. For cross-generator scenarios where Llama generates data but Qwen trains, the adaptive approach still works similarly well, though the detector might need retraining to handle the distribution shift.\n\nWhen I try hard thresholding at q=0.5 to remove synthetic data outright, it discards about 40% of the training set, which actually hurts performance compared to adaptive weighting—the borderline samples contain useful signal. Random reweighting performs no better than uniform weighting, confirming that the detector signal itself is what drives the improvement.\n\nMoving to P2, I'm testing domain-skewed contamination where synthetic data clusters in code, and the adaptive method handles this well by adjusting the reweighting per batch. I'm also running downstream evaluations on MMLU and HellaSwag with Llama-3.2-3B to see how these improvements translate to actual task performance.\n\nNow I'm looking at the aggregate ratio calculation—the best tier should show [95, 115] but the MVP data gives 88.6%. I need to reconsider how this ratio changes when I scale to 60% contamination on the 3B model, since that's where adaptive might outperform the grid-based baseline and push the aggregate ratio higher. Now I'm ready to write out the full YAML configuration. I need to think through which experiments are already done versus which ones are still planned — looking at the registry, I can see that the synthetic data generation, baseline uniform runs, the TF-IDF detector, and the initial adaptive experiments with n_min=3 are all completed, but there are still several grid search and newer adaptive variants to document. Now I'm organizing the full experiment pipeline across three priority levels, noting that setup and data generation steps will track data quality metrics rather than training performance. I'm ready to construct the comprehensive YAML configuration.\n\nWriting YAML structure... they don't produce training metrics.\n\nAlso, looking at the format more carefully — each experiment needs: name, plan_id, description, expected_results, result_rationale, tables, figures_description, analysis.\n\nLet me also be careful about the numbers. Let me anchor everything to existing data.\n\nExisting data summary:\n- Uniform PPL (40%, Qwen3-0.6B): s42=17.690, s123=17.690, s456=17.700\n- Grid-best b=0.5 PPL: s42=17.651, s123=17.638, s456=17.650\n- Grid b=1.0 PPL: s42=17.692, s123=17.674, s456=17.680\n- Grid b=1.5 PPL: s42=17.718, s123=17.701, s456=17.710\n- Grid b=2.0 PPL: s42=17.741, s123=17.725\n\nNow I'm filling in the higher b values and the adaptive variant. The pattern shows PPL degrading as b increases, with the adaptive approach performing best. For the finer grid around the optimum, I expect b=0.1 to show gentle improvement over uniform, b=0.2 to improve further, and b=0.3 to approach the b=0.5 performance, creating a broad minimum in that range.\n\nLooking at the actual improvements from uniform baseline: b=0.5 gives about 0.039 PPL reduction, and I'm seeing b=0.3 capture roughly 92% of that gain, b=0.2 about 59%, and b=0.1 about 46%. The b=1.0 result is interesting — it's actually slightly worse than uniform, suggesting we've overshot the optimal range.\n\nChecking across the other seeds, the pattern shifts: seed123 and seed456 both show b=1.0 still beating uniform, though by smaller margins (0.016 and 0.020 respectively). This tells me the optimal b value and the sharpness of the curve vary depending on the seed. For seed42 the minimum is tightly peaked around b=0.5, but for the other seeds it's broader and extends further.\n\nNow looking at the finer grid results for seed42, I'm seeing the best tier cluster around b=0.2 to b=0.3, with b=0.1 at 17.681, b=0.2 at 17.668, and b=0.3 at 17.655.\n\nAcross all three seeds, b=0.3 comes close to b=0.5 but doesn't consistently outperform it—b=0.5 remains the strongest, or the optimal region plateaus somewhere between 0.3 and 0.5. This confirms b=0.5 wasn't sitting at a boundary of the search space.\n\nFor the best tier, the hypothesis states that b=0.5 is the true optimum with no significant gains from a finer grid between 0.1 and 0.4. So b=0.3 should be competitive but not meaningfully better. I'll have b=0.3 marginally edge out b=0.5 in a couple of seeds, but the differences stay within typical seed variance—small enough that they don't constitute a real improvement.\n\nLooking at the cross-seed results, b=0.3 and b=0.5 average nearly identically around 17.645-17.646, confirming they're essentially tied and that b=0.5 sits comfortably within the optimal plateau rather than at a boundary. This supports the narrative well: the broad optimum region means the exact b choice matters little, which validates using an adaptive approach that naturally covers this range. I should stick with b=0.5 as the grid-best since b=0.3 doesn't offer a meaningful improvement. The aggregate ratio of 88.6% seems low for the best tier, but that's because it's calculated across all settings—when I factor in the 60% contamination cases where adaptive can outperform grid-best, plus the 3B model results where they're closely matched, the overall aggregate ratio climbs above 95%. Let me be more precise about what aggregate means: it's the average ratio of adaptive PPL improvement to grid-best PPL improvement computed across every setting and seed combination.\n\nLooking at the breakdown, the 40% Qwen-0.6B results across three seeds average to 91%, the 60% Qwen case hits around 110%, and the 40% Llama-3B setting lands near 100%, which when combined gives me that 96.6% overall—right in the target range.\n\nNow I'm checking the cross-setting win rate metric, which measures how often adaptive outperforms uniform across individual comparisons. Looking at the specific matchups across different seeds and model configurations, adaptive is winning consistently across all the test cases I'm examining.\n\nFor the best tier rating, I'd want a perfect win rate of 1.0, meaning adaptive beats uniform in every single setting without exception.\n\nI'm also evaluating the adaptive_within_seed_var_of_gridbest_fraction metric, which should fall between 0.85 and 1.0 to indicate that most seeds have adaptive performance within the natural variance of the grid-best baseline. From my current data, 3 out of 3 seeds appear to be within this variance range, though one is borderline given the grid-best variance of about 0.01-0.015 PPL and the adaptive-grid gap ranging from 0.001-0.017. For a best-tier result, I'd expect most settings to clearly fall within seed variance.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile the full YAML output organized by plan_id for the datagen and setup experiments.\n\nFor the training experiments, I'll need to be much more thorough. Let me reconsider the 60% contamination scenario more carefully. At 40% contamination, uniform PPL sits around 17.69 with only about 0.25% relative improvement from grid-best, but at 60% contamination the baseline gets worse due to more synthetic data. The PPL degradation likely scales nonlinearly—if pure real data is around 17.5 PPL, then 40% contamination adds roughly 0.19 PPL (1.1% degradation) while 60% would add about 0.35 PPL (2.0% degradation). With more contamination, grid-best should have a larger effect since there are more samples to downweight, though the benefit might plateau as the contamination becomes too severe.\n\nAt 60% contamination, grid-best shifts to b=1.5 (more aggressive than the b=0.5 at 40%) and achieves 17.72 PPL, giving a 0.13 PPL improvement (0.73% relative) compared to the 0.04 PPL improvement at 40% (0.23% relative)—that's roughly a 3.2x amplification, which fits the expected 3-5x range. The adaptive method actually outperforms grid-best in the best tier at 60% contamination, reaching 17.70 PPL versus grid-best's 17.72. by 0.02)\n- Ratio: (17.85 - 17.70) / (17.85 - 17.72) = 0.15 / 0.13 = 115%\n\nThat's at the upper bound of aggregate_ratio_pct range. Let me tone it down slightly:\n- Adaptive PPL: 17.71\n- Ratio: 0.14 / 0.13 = 107.7%\n\nFor the grid-b=0.5 at 60%:\n- b=0.5 at 60% should be suboptimal (too gentle for 60% contamination)\n- PPL: 17.78 (better than uniform 17.85 but worse than optimal b=1.5)\n\nFor b* distribution at 60%:\n- Median should shift to ~1.3-1.5 (from 0.87 at 40%)\n- This tracks the shift in grid\n\nNow moving to the 3B model, which has roughly 5x more parameters than the 1.7B. It should show lower baseline PPL due to greater capacity, but also demonstrate heightened sensitivity to contamination since more parameters can memorize. The reweighting improvements should be more pronounced. I'm estimating clean PPL around 11.5-12.5 and with 40% contamination around 12.5-13.0, so I'll set uniform PPL at 12.80 for the 3B at 40% contamination. For the optimal b value, the 1.7B used b=1, so the 3B likely needs something in the b=1-2 range depending on how the scaling affects optimal regularization. I'm fine-tuning the adaptive PPL at 3B to hit exactly 110% of the improvement ratio—12.69 works perfectly. For the b* distribution, I'm expecting the median to cluster around 1.0-1.2 for 3B, which contrasts with the 0.6B model's median of ~0.87 and shows the method adapts across model scales. Now I'm checking the downstream task performance, starting with MMLU and HellaSwag scores at 3B. , not great)\n- MMLU with adaptive: ~32.5-33% (0.5-1% absolute improvement)\n- HellaSwag at 3B with uniform: ~52%\n- HellaSwag with adaptive: ~52.8-53.5% (0.8-1.5% improvement)\n\nWithin the downstream_mmlu_delta_abs_pct range of [0.3, 1.5].\n\nFor n_min sensitivity:\nn_min=1: Very low ESS constraint → b* can go very high → some batches get over-downweighted\n- PPL: 17.670 (worse than n_min=2's 17.650 but better than uniform 17.690)\n- b* distribution: more spread out, higher median (~1.5-2.0)\n\nn_min=2 (existing): PPL = 17.650 (best performance)\n\nn_min=3 and higher values show degradation, with PPL climbing back to 17.690 as n_min approaches or exceeds the batch size, forcing b* toward zero and reverting to uniform weighting. The ablation clearly shows n_min=2 hits the sweet spot. For the detector comparison, ModernBERT achieves much higher AUC than TF-IDF, though the perplexity improvement with ModernBERT is marginal, suggesting a simpler detector works nearly as well.\n\nIn cross-generator scenarios where Llama generates synthetic data for Qwen training, the TF-IDF detector needs retraining, and while adaptive weighting still helps, the gains are comparable to same-generator cases—around 0.05 PPL improvement. When applying detect-and-remove with a threshold that filters out roughly 40% of data, training on the remaining 60% (real data plus borderline synthetic examples) shows diminishing returns.\n\nLooking at the detect-and-remove results, the